<p>Iran’s leadership is entering a sensitive transition as the country’s Assembly of Experts considers who should become the next Supreme Leader—a role with decisive authority over state institutions and the armed forces. Multiple outlets report that Mojtaba Khamenei, the son of Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, has emerged as a leading contender; his low public profile and reported ties to Iran’s security establishment feature prominently in assessments of his influence. A succession within the Khamenei family is drawing scrutiny in Iran and abroad because Iran’s post-1979 system is not meant to be hereditary, and past criticism has warned that such an outcome could look like a religious version of the monarchy the revolution overthrew. At the same time, some reporting says other names are circulating—including former president Hassan Rouhani—underscoring the uncertainty and political maneuvering around the process. The outcome is still unclear, but the transition also offers a chance for Iran’s institutions to show they can manage a high-stakes handover in an orderly way, with the Assembly of Experts’ constitutional role providing a formal path for leadership change.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/IlgdFPVYtFNvJW4XhMnxH2c2v6FZucCwooKV0G7eegfTHZ4xEA5M719aRbyJgJteEFpQ7HrIYyp21l1eSZptj6RnWXkeGYtygQrxIlVK9xymuFua7JRUcl3pPr08hKZYNT2eSyKhtSuNCHKTpcbQyi0K7XaSPx_XE28TX829uok1NikCtdadrxrLXTZPApiYmHOddtmehysOVkXRBRL--_2i59owiuUnaisPZYHeCfi64au-dVJF3Pzs-rJI6BhXv861_wF-9FKoB_Lhuw' alt='Mojtaba Khamenei participates in the annual Quds Day rally in Tehran, May 31, 2019.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Selection body: Iran’s Assembly of Experts chooses the next Supreme Leader, putting its deliberations at the heart of the transition.</li><li>Long-running speculation: PBS says Mojtaba Khamenei’s name has circulated for years as a possible successor to his father.</li><li>No official post: One account says Mojtaba Khamenei has never been elected or appointed to a 